WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:13.650
The carriage stopped in front of a modest-looking courthouse.

2
00:00:13.651 --> 00:00:19.069
Mr. Fogg, Passepartout, and Aouda were taken into a room with barred windows.

3
00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:25.309
"You will appear before Judge Obadiah at half past eight," said the policeman.

4
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:33.309
After the policeman had left them and closed the door, Passepartout fell into a chair and exclaimed, "Why are we prisoners?"

5
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:39.901
Trying to conceal her emotions, Aouda, said to Mr. Fogg, "Sir, you must leave me to my fate!

6
00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:43.866
You have been arrested because of me—because you saved me!"

7
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:47.867
Phileas Fogg explained that this was impossible.

8
00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:53.106
It was unlikely that he would be arrested for having prevented a human sacrifice.

9
00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:58.507
He would not, in any event, abandon Aouda but would escort her to Hong Kong.

10
00:00:58.508 --> 00:01:03.924
"But the steamer leaves at noon!" observed Passepartout nervously.

11
00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:08.161
"And we shall be on board by noon," replied Fogg.

12
00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:13.691
He said it so positively that Passepartout couldn't help but be reassured.

13
00:01:13.692 --> 00:01:16.702
At half past eight, the door opened.

14
00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:20.619
The policeman reappeared and instructed them to follow.

15
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:28.987
He led them to a courtroom. Mr. Fogg and his two companions entered and took their places at a desk opposite the judge.

16
00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:38.149
Passepartout was getting more nervous. On the clock over the judge's head, the hands seemed to be going around very, very quickly.

17
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:43.831
Judge Obadiah was a fat, round man who wore an old, white wig.

18
00:01:43.832 --> 00:01:49.031
"The first case, Mr. Phileas Fogg," he said.

19
00:01:49.032 --> 00:01:51.864
"I am here," replied Mr. Fogg.

20
00:01:51.865 --> 00:01:54.785
"Passepartout?" asked the judge.

21
00:01:54.786 --> 00:01:57.806
"Present," responded Passepartout.

22
00:01:57.807 --> 00:02:05.735
"Good," said the judge. "Authorities have been searching for you for two days on the train from Bombay."

23
00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:10.484
A door swung open, and three Indian priests entered.

24
00:02:10.485 --> 00:02:16.179
The priests took their places in front of the judge, and the clerk of the court stood.

25
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.995
In a loud voice, he read a complaint of sacrilege against Phileas Fogg and his servant,

26
00:02:20.996 --> 00:02:27.166
who were accused of having violated a place held sacred by the Brahmin religion.

27
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:30.577
"You hear the charge?" asked the judge.

28
00:02:30.578 --> 00:02:36.205
"Yes, sir," replied Mr. Fogg, consulting his watch, "and I admit to it."

29
00:02:36.206 --> 00:02:38.992
"You admit it?" asked the judge.

30
00:02:38.993 --> 00:02:47.218
"Yes," said Fogg. "And I wish to hear these priests admit to what they were going to do at the pagoda."

31
00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:52.317
The priests looked at each other, not seeming to understand what he was saying.

32
00:02:52.318 --> 00:02:59.107
"At the pagoda of Pillaji," said Passepartout. "They were on the point of burning their victim."

33
00:02:59.108 --> 00:03:03.865
The judge stared with astonishment, and the priests were shocked.

34
00:03:03.866 --> 00:03:12.015
"What victim?" asked the judge. "We are talking about the pagoda on Malabar Hill, in Bombay."

35
00:03:12.016 --> 00:03:15.760
The judge placed a pair of shoes on his desk.

36
00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:20.202
"My shoes!" cried Passepartout in surprise.

37
00:03:20.203 --> 00:03:27.942
In all the confusion since they had left Bombay, Phileas Fogg and his servant had quite forgotten the affair over Passepartout's shoes.

38
00:03:27.943 --> 00:03:34.086
They also did not notice the detective, Fix, watching from a corner of the courtroom.

39
00:03:34.087 --> 00:03:39.606
Fix had foreseen how Passepartout's misadventure could detain Mr. Fogg in Calcutta.

40
00:03:39.607 --> 00:03:48.042
He had gone to talk with the priests on Malabar Hill, promised them a large sum of money, and sent them to Calcutta on the next train.

41
00:03:48.043 --> 00:03:55.437
The priests had arrived ahead of Mr. Fogg and Passepartout, however, and Fix had been feverishly watching the train station in Calcutta.

42
00:03:55.438 --> 00:04:02.686
When Fix had seen Mr. Fogg and Passepartout disembark that morning, he had immediately found a policeman to arrest the men.

43
00:04:02.687 --> 00:04:09.066
Judge Obadiah had heard Passepartout's outburst about his shoes and said, "The facts are admitted.

44
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:16.450
I condemn this man, Passepartout, to imprisonment for fifteen days and a fine of three hundred pounds.

45
00:04:16.451 --> 00:04:24.277
I further condemn Mr. Fogg to a week's imprisonment and a fine of one hundred and fifty pounds."

46
00:04:24.278 --> 00:04:27.334
Fix rubbed his hands with satisfaction.

47
00:04:27.335 --> 00:04:35.021
If Phileas Fogg could be detained in Calcutta for a week, it would be enough time for the arrest warrant to arrive from London.

48
00:04:35.022 --> 00:04:40.220
Phileas Fogg, as self-composed as ever, said, "I offer bail."

49
00:04:40.221 --> 00:04:42.855
Fix's blood ran cold.

50
00:04:42.856 --> 00:04:46.769
"You have that right," said the judge.

51
00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:52.842
"I will pay it at once," said Mr. Fogg, taking a roll of banknotes from the carpetbag.

52
00:04:52.843 --> 00:04:57.890
Bail was set at two thousand pounds, and Mr. Fogg handed the money to the clerk.

53
00:04:57.891 --> 00:05:07.746
"This sum will be restored to you at the end of your sentence," said the judge. "Meanwhile you are liberated on bail."

54
00:05:07.747 --> 00:05:11.289
"Come!" said Phileas Fogg to his servant.

55
00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.666
He offered his arm to Aouda and they departed.

56
00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:23.119
Fix followed them to the quay. His only hope was that Fogg would not be willing to leave the two thousand pounds behind him.

57
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.411
The steamer departing for Hong Kong was the Rangoon.

58
00:05:27.412 --> 00:05:31.954
A flag flying from its mast signaled that it was about to depart.

59
00:05:31.955 --> 00:05:41.947
Fix watched as Fogg and his companions got out of their carriage and pushed off in a boat for the steamer. Fix stamped his feet in disappointment.

60
00:05:41.948 --> 00:05:48.539
"The rascal is off after all!" he said. "Two thousand pounds sacrificed!

61
00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.154
I'll follow him to the end of the world if necessary.

62
00:05:52.155 --> 00:06:00.228
But at the rate Fogg is going, the money he stole will soon be exhausted!"

63
00:06:00.229 --> 00:06:05.431
That was a close one! Thank goodness Mr. Fogg was able to post bail.

64
00:06:05.432 --> 00:06:11.535
But I must be careful. My mistakes are costing him so much money!

65
00:06:11.536 --> 00:06:17.328
I couldn't help noticing that Judge Obadiah's wig and clothes seemed royal, like something a king would wear.

66
00:06:17.329 --> 00:06:23.293
Interestingly, I learned that this costume does have links to royalty—French royalty!

67
00:06:23.294 --> 00:06:28.609
In the sixteenth century, King Louis XIII, of France, began wearing a wig.

68
00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:33.042
It didn't take long for all wealthy aristocrats to start wearing wigs too.

69
00:06:33.043 --> 00:06:37.919
However, by the eighteenth century, wigs became less and less popular.

70
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.313
Today in England lawyers and judges are the only people who still wear them.

71
00:06:42.314 --> 00:06:49.313
And that's fine with me. I think I look perfect like this anyway!
